.Dd September 24, 2026
.Dt PASSWD 1
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm passwd
.Nd change a password
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl dlu
.Op Fl i Cm files | directory
.Op Ar user
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
changes a password.
With no arguments it changes your own.
Given a
.Ar user ,
it changes theirs, which only root may do.
.Pp
NextBSD keeps regular accounts in the DirectoryServices property lists and
system accounts in
.Pa /etc/master.passwd .
.Nm
edits an account
.Em wherever it already lives
and does not choose by uid: an account that exists has already settled which
store it is in.
.Xr adduser 8
and
.Xr pw 8
are the tools that decide where a
.Em new
account goes.
.Pp
A session looks like this:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
Changing local password for joe.
Old password:
New password:
Retype new password:
passwd: password updated
.Ed
.Pp
The old password is not asked for when root is making the change, or when the
account has no password to prove.
An empty new password leaves the account alone and prints
.Dq Li Password unchanged.
A new password that does not match its confirmation prints
.Dq Li Mismatch; try again, EOF to quit.
and asks again.
.Ss Privilege
.Nm
is installed setuid root, because the property lists are readable by everyone
and writable only by root.
Every decision about what you are allowed to change is made from your
.Em real
user id; the effective one is used for nothing but the write.
.Ss A joined machine
When this machine is bound to a directory server, its accounts come from the
server's copy of the property lists and
.Nm
refuses to change them.
The password belongs to the server and must be changed there.
.Pp
Bound means
.Pa /Network/Library/DirectoryServices/Domain.plist
is present, and it is asked only after
.Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Domain.plist :
on the server itself
.Nm
changes the password here, because that is where it lives.
See
.Xr dspromote 8
for the whole rule.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-i files|directory"
.It Fl d
Give the account no password at all, so it logs in without one.
Root only, and only for an account in the property lists.
.It Fl l
Lock the account.
The stored hash is prefixed with
.Ql *LOCKED* ,
which no password can produce, so nothing authenticates until it is unlocked.
Root only, and only for an account in the property lists.
.It Fl u
Unlock an account locked with
.Fl l ,
restoring the password it had before.
Root only.
.It Fl i Cm files | directory
Act on that store instead of looking for the account.
.Cm files
means
.Pa /etc/master.passwd ,
.Cm directory
means the property lists.
For repairing a machine where an account exists in both.
.El
.Pp
.Fl d ,
.Fl l
and
.Fl u
are refused for an account in
.Pa /etc/master.passwd .
That file has its own conventions for a disabled account and
.Xr vipw 8
is the tool for them; doing something almost-equivalent here would be worse
than refusing.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Users.plist" -compact
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Users.plist
regular accounts on this machine
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Domain.plist
present when this machine is the directory server
.It Pa /Network/Library/DirectoryServices/Domain.plist
present when this machine is bound to a server
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Binding.plist
names that server; it does not decide the role
.It Pa /etc/master.passwd
root and system accounts
.It Pa /etc/login.conf
.Va passwd_format
here chooses the hash format; the default is
.Li sha512
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
An empty new password is not a failure: the account is left alone and the exit
status is 0.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Change your own password:
.Pp
.Dl $ passwd
.Pp
Give the seeded administrator a password, which also stops the console logging
in automatically:
.Pp
.Dl # passwd admin
.Pp
Give root a password, so it can log in directly rather than only through
.Xr sudo 8 :
.Pp
.Dl # passwd root
.Pp
Lock an account without destroying the password it had:
.Pp
.Dl # passwd -l joe
.Dl # passwd -u joe
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chpass 1 ,
.Xr login.conf 5 ,
.Xr adduser 8 ,
.Xr pw 8 ,
.Xr vipw 8
.Sh HISTORY
This
.Nm
was written for NextBSD, to change a password in the DirectoryServices
property lists as well as in
.Pa /etc/master.passwd .
The one it replaces could not: it decided what it was willing to edit from the
source an account was resolved from, and refused anything that was not a flat
file or NIS.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Joseph Maloney
